# Overview
#
# A vague criterion is a word that leaves the reader to supply a
# criterion the writer knows (`choose an appropriate timeout`). The fix
# names the criterion (`choose a timeout longer than the slowest
# request`) or drops the word. The rule sits at Optional, because a word
# rule cannot see a criterion that the sentence names a few words later.
# Human-written docs also use these words about three times as often as
# the phrases of the Recommended ProseVagueCriterion.
#
# Expression
#
# `fit` holds `appropriate`, and `word` holds the other words, such as
# `properly`, `suitable`, and `reasonable`. Each word counts only
# without `for` or `to` after it. A space in a pattern is a whitespace
# token, so each guard sits glued to its word and reads the token right
# after it.
#
# `reasonably` counts only before an adjective or an adverb (`reasonably
# fast`). An adverb is a word that describes a verb, an adjective, or a
# whole sentence, such as `safely` or `also`. Harper's part-of-speech
# tagger marks each word with a tag for its word class. The pattern
# names the tag for an adjective (`ADJ`) and the tag for an adverb
# (`ADV`).
#
# `appropriate` does not count after `as`, `where`, `when`, or `if`.
# Weir has no lookbehind, so the filter consumes the word before
# `appropriate` and narrows the finding back to `appropriate`. A
# sentence that opens with `Appropriate` therefore passes.
#
# False Positives
#
# `for` and `to` are the two words that most often introduce the
# criterion, so a word passes before either one (`suitable for
# bootstrapping`). `proper` is left out, because it names a math term
# (`a proper subset`). `relevant` is left out, because it usually points
# at a named thing (`the relevant API reference`). Before a verb,
# `reasonably` means plausibly (`you might reasonably expect`), so it
# passes there.
#
# The rule still reports a word whose criterion the sentence names a few
# words later (`an appropriate label matching topologyKey`).
#
# Related Rules
#
# The Recommended ProseVagueCriterion holds the phrases (`as needed`,
# `where applicable`), and the Optional ProseVagueCriterion holds the
# single words (`appropriate`, `properly`).
#
# The Recommended ProseVagueCriterion reports `as appropriate`, `where
# appropriate`, `when appropriate`, and `if appropriate`. One word
# should draw one finding, so `appropriate` passes here after `as`,
# `where`, `when`, or `if`. No other word in this rule has that guard.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 224 lines in the measure.py corpus, and about 15 of 25
# sampled hits left the criterion unstated.
expr word [appropriately, properly, suitable, suitably, reasonable, sensible, sane]
expr fit [appropriate]
expr main [<(![as, where, when, if] @fit!(( )[for, to])), @fit>, (@word!(( )[for, to])), (reasonably [ADJ, ADV])]
let message "Vague criterion. Name the condition that makes it right, or drop the word."
let description "Flags `appropriate`, `properly`, `reasonable`, and similar words that leave the reader to supply a criterion."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "The image is suitable for bootstrapping the control plane."
allows "The limit is appropriate to a single tenant."
allows "Choose a timeout longer than the slowest request."
allows "Restructure the manuals where appropriate."
allows "You might reasonably expect the cache to be warm."
